<BaseLayout title="Water Damage Drying Equipment Explained | UNITED" description="Learn how extraction, air movers, dehumidifiers, moisture meters, and HEPA filtration support professional structural drying."><article class="article-page shell"><p class="eyebrow">Field note</p><h1>What tools help dry a water-damaged structure?</h1><p class="lede">Professional drying is a system. No single machine replaces inspection, moisture mapping, extraction, or monitoring.</p><h2>Extraction equipment</h2><p>Extraction removes accessible liquid water before evaporation takes over. That reduces the burden on the drying environment.</p><h2>Air movers</h2><p>Air movement supports evaporation at wet surfaces. Placement depends on the affected materials and the path air can take.</p><h2>Commercial dehumidifiers</h2><p>As materials release moisture, dehumidification removes it from the surrounding air and helps keep conditions controlled.</p><h2>Moisture meters</h2><p>Material readings help define the affected area and track change. They provide better guidance than appearance or touch alone.</p><h2>HEPA filtration</h2><p>Filtration may be used when the work plan calls for airborne particulate control. Its role depends on the scope.</p><a class="button primary" href="/contact/">Request service</a></article></BaseLayout>
